+<section class="pagehead"><div class="wrap"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumbs"><a href="en.html">Home</a><span>/</span>Buying property from a non-resident in Spain: 3% withholding and form 211</nav><h1>Buying property from a non-resident in Spain: 3% withholding and form 211</h1><p class="ru-note"><a href="comprar-inmueble-a-no-residente-modelo-211.html" hreflang="es" lang="es">Leer en español →</a> · <a href="fr-acheter-vendeur-non-resident-modele-211.html" hreflang="fr" lang="fr">Français</a> · <a href="de-kauf-nichtansaessiger-verkaeufer-modelo-211.html" hreflang="de" lang="de">Deutsch</a></p><p class="lead"><strong>Grupo Avanza Consultores reviews and prepares the buyer’s withholding and form 211 when Spanish property is sold by a non-resident without a permanent establishment.</strong> Based in Paterna, Valencia, we work remotely throughout Spain, with in-person appointments by arrangement. We check the seller’s tax residence, price, ownership shares and transfer date. We reply in writing in English. <a href="mailto:avanza@grupoavanzaconsultores.es">Email us before signing</a>.</p><div class="ctas"><a class="btn btn--primary" href="en-contact.html?pagina=buying-property-from-non-resident-spain.html">Request a quote</a><a class="btn btn--ghost" href="#servicio">What we review</a></div></div></section>
 <section class="band" id="servicio" style="padding-top:64px"><div class="wrap--narrow prose">
 <h2>Address the buyer’s obligation before completion</h2><p>When a non-resident income taxpayer transfers Spanish property without a permanent establishment, the buyer must withhold and pay over 3% of the agreed consideration, or make the corresponding payment on account (article 25.2 of the Non-Resident Income Tax Act and article 14.1 of its Regulations). The seller’s tax position determines whether this applies. The Tax Agency confirms that both resident and non-resident buyers can have this obligation.</p><p>The withholding is a payment on account of the seller’s tax, rather than their final liability. It is distinct from purchase taxes and other transaction costs. We therefore review the evidence before the purchase payment is finalised.</p>
 <h2>The seller’s residence needs evidence</h2><p>Nationality, a Spanish address on the deed or a NIE does not settle the check. Article 14.2.a of the Regulations excludes withholding where the seller proves liability to Spanish Personal Income Tax or Corporation Tax through a certificate from the competent tax authority. We review that certificate and the transaction before concluding that withholding is not required.</p><p>The Regulations also provide an exception for contributions of property on the formation or increase of the capital of companies resident in Spain (article 14.2.b). An ordinary purchase is not treated as a capital contribution.</p>
