 <section class="band" id="servicio" style="padding-top:64px"><div class="wrap--narrow prose">
 <h2>Your tax residence and use of the property come first</h2><p>Living abroad, holding a NIE or receiving rent into a foreign bank account does not provide all the information needed for the return. We check tax residence for the period, entitlement to rent and any combination of letting and personal use. Income from property located in Spain is Spanish-source income (article 13.1.g of the Non-Resident Income Tax Act). This page covers letting without a permanent establishment; other services or a business organisation need to be assessed first.</p>
 <h2>Income, expenses and tax rate</h2><p>The general rule in article 24.1 of the Non-Resident Income Tax Act starts with gross income. Under article 24.6, individuals resident in the European Union may deduct expenses allowed under the Personal Income Tax Act when they prove a direct connection with the income and a direct, inseparable economic link with the activity in Spain. This extends to the European Economic Area on that provision’s effective-exchange-of-information terms. An invoice or bank charge alone does not prove an expense meets those requirements.</p><p>The general rate is 24%. It is 19% for residents of another European Union Member State or a European Economic Area state with effective exchange of tax information (article 25.1.a). We establish the appropriate tax base and rate from your evidence. The rate alone is not the final amount due.</p>
diff --git a/self-employed-autonomo-spain-foreigner.html b/self-employed-autonomo-spain-foreigner.html
