 <p class="ru-note"><a href="autonomo-extranjero-en-espana.html" hreflang="es" lang="es">Esta guía en español →</a> · <a href="ru-autonomo-dve-strany-es.html" hreflang="ru" lang="ru">Это руководство на русском →</a></p>
 <p class="lead">To register as autónomo (self-employed) in Spain as a foreigner you need a Spanish tax identification number, which for a foreign national is the NIE (if you have no NIE, you must ask the Spanish Tax Agency to assign you a tax identification number), and you must file two registrations before you start the activity: the census declaration with the Spanish Tax Agency and registration in the self-employed Social Security scheme (RETA), which is filed online and no earlier than 60 calendar days before the planned start. EU and EEA citizens may work as self-employed on the same terms as Spanish nationals; as a rule, other nationals, except family members of EU or EEA citizens to whom Royal Decree 240/2007 grants the same right and those who already hold an authorisation that allows self-employed work, first need a residence and work authorisation for self-employed activity, an immigration procedure that we do not handle. If you already work in another EU country, which country’s Social Security legislation applies has to be settled first, as explained below.</p><p class="lead"><strong>Grupo Avanza Consultores, a tax and accounting adviser in Paterna, Valencia, reviews the position of UK and EU freelancers living in Spain: where you are tax resident, whether and when you register as autónomo, and which country’s Social Security applies before you file anything. We work online for clients across Spain, in written English by email, with a quote before work starts.</strong></p>
 <p>We offer written support in English, Spanish, French, German and Russian.</p>
